OpenDUNE for the Sinclair QL with the QLion Gold card
=====================================================

This is a port of OpenDUNE, the open source re-creation of Westwood's
Dune II, to the Sinclair QL fitted with a QLion Gold card. It uses the
card's 256-colour screen, PS/2 mouse, digital sound and YM2149.

OpenDUNE is licensed under the GNU General Public License version 2.
This port is too; its full source is published alongside the program.
It is not affiliated with Westwood Studios, Virgin or the owners of Dune II.


What you need
-------------

* A Sinclair QL with a QLion Gold card.
* A PS/2 mouse on the QLion card.
* The data files from your own copy of DUNE II, version 1.07 (EU, US or
  HitSquad release). They are NOT included and must not be shared:

      ATRE.PAK    DUNE.PAK     ENGLISH.PAK  FINALE.PAK   HARK.PAK
      HERC.PAK    INTRO.PAK    INTROVOC.PAK MENTAT.PAK   MERC.PAK
      ORDOS.PAK   OVERLAY.PAK  SCENARIO.PAK SOUND.PAK    VOC.PAK
      DUNE2.EXE

  The EU release may also have FRENCH.PAK and GERMAN.PAK. Files of the
  older version 1.0 mostly work but are not supported; the official
  Westwood 1.07 patch updates them.

* The DUNE II manual: like the original, the game asks a question from
  it at certain points (copy protection).


Installing
----------

1. Copy opendune and all the files listed above into one directory,
   for example WIN1_DUNE2_ or SDA1_DUNE2_. Keep the dots in the file
   names (DUNE.PAK, not DUNE_PAK).

2. Make that directory the default data directory and start the game:

      DATA_USE WIN1_DUNE2_
      EW WIN1_DUNE2_opendune

   Only a 1000 data space is needed if it is needed to use fixexe or another 
   program to restore a lost header.

3. Do not load the Pointer Environment's mouse driver before starting:
   the game reads the QLion mouse itself.

Savegames, OPTIONS.CFG and DUNE.CFG are written to the same directory.
I recommend a small volume for music and drums or dissable music for
better sounding digital audio.

Options (opendune.ini)
----------------------

An optional text file opendune.ini in the game directory can change some
settings. The game finds it through DATA_USE, like all its other files, so
DATA_USE must point to the game directory. All lines go under an
[opendune] section:

      [opendune]
      mousespeed=130     mouse speed in percent (10 - 1000), default 130
      music=0            AY music: 0 = off (default), 1 = on
      musicvolume=60     music volume in percent (0 - 400), default 60
      drumvolume=50      drum volume in percent of the music volume,
                         default 50
      showfps=0          1 = show screen updates per second, top left
      hwcursor=0         1 = draw the mouse pointer with a QLion hardware
                         sprite (smoother; moves in 2-pixel steps)
      soundbuffer=16     sound effects and voices: 16 = 16 KB sample
                         buffer (default; current QLion firmware and the
                         standalone cores), 8 = 8 KB buffer (older QLion
                         firmware), 0 = off. The standalone cores always
                         use 16 KB.
      soundvolume=100    volume of sound effects and voices in percent
                         (0 - 100), default 100

The standard OpenDUNE options (dune2_enhanced, debug_skip_dialogs ...)
work as on other platforms.


Controls
--------

Mouse as in the original game: left button selects and orders, right
button cancels. Keyboard shortcuts work as usual (the first letter of a
command); the cursor keys scroll the map.


Hardware used
-------------

* Screen: QLion mode 33, 512x256 in 256 colours (RRRGGGBB) at $4C0000.
  The 320x200 game screen is centred and converted from the game's
  palette on the fly, so palette fades work without palette registers.
* Mouse: QIMI-compatible registers, read by an external interrupt server.
  With hwcursor=1 the pointer is shown with hardware sprites 0 and 1.
* Sound effects and voices: 16 KB (or 8 KB, soundbuffer=8) stereo
  sample buffer at $C04000.
* Music (music=1): the game's MIDI music played on the YM2149 (3 voices).
  The drums are played as samples on the sample buffer (they pause while
  an effect or voice plays); with soundbuffer=0 they fall back to the YM
  noise generator.
* Timing: the QLion 1 MHz counter 1 ($800030).


Building from source
--------------------

Cross-compiled on a PC with qdos-gcc:

      make -f Makefile.ql

Makefile.ql lists all QL-specific files at the top.
